    "https://recruit.ap.ucsb.edu/JPF03150": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Researcher in Gray Whale Ecology and Biology, Marine Science Institute, UC Santa BarbaraApply nowto Postdoctoral Researcher in Gray Whale Ecology and Biology, Marine Science Institute, UC Santa Barbara",
      "description": "Postdoctoral Researcher in Gray Whale Ecology and Biology, Marine Science Institute, UC Santa Barbara (JPF03150) - Recruit Skip to main content Recruit Home Open Recruitments Postdoctoral Researcher in Gray Whale Ecology and Biology, Marine Science Institute, UC Santa Barbara (JPF03150) Postdoctoral Researcher in Gray Whale Ecology and Biology, Marine Science Institute, UC Santa Barbara Apply now to Postdoctoral Researcher in Gray Whale Ecology and Biology, Marine Science Institute, UC Santa Barbara Job #JPF03150 Marine Science Institute / Office of Research / UC Santa Barbara Position overview Position title: Postdoctoral Researcher in Gray Whale Ecology and Biology Salary range: A reasonable estimate for this position at 100% time is $71,491 to $85,736. Percent time: 100% Anticipated start: Position start date is negotiable. It is anticipated to ideally start in Fall 2026 or later. Position duration: The initial appointment is for 2 years from the start date. Application Window Open date: August 31, 2026 Next review date: Wednesday, Sep 30, 2026 at 11:59pm (Pacific Time) Apply by this date to ensure full consideration by the committee. Final date: Friday, Apr 30, 2027 at 11:59pm (Pacific Time) Applications will continue to be accepted until this date, but those received after the review date will only be considered if the position has not yet been filled. Position description The Benioff Ocean Science Laboratory seeks a highly motivated postdoctoral scholar to conduct field research on gray whale biology and ecology in the San Francisco Bay. In the last five years, an increasing number of gray whales (Eschrichtius robustus) appear to be using the Bay as a migratory stopover during their 12,000 mile journey between Mexico and the Arctic. Marine mammal biologists have observed gray whales feeding and/or attempting to feed in the Bay, a departure from past migratory behavior that is not yet well understood and that puts whales in closer contact with human activity and often at greater risk of harm, including increased risk of ship strikes. This research is targeted at answering some of the core ecological questions related to understanding how and why gray whales are currently using the Bay and what effects this use of Bay habitats has on the whales themselves. Though specific research questions may be defined in collaboration with the successful candidate, example questions that may be addressed include: ● What San Francisco Bay habitats are most important to gray whales and what are the general temporal and spatial patterns of use? ● What specific benthic prey resources are the whales using in the Bay? ● What is the nutritional value of these prey resources and is there physiological evidence that gray whales are benefiting from access to these food resources? ● To which toxicological risks could gray whales be exposed by feeding upon prey sources within the Bay? ● How may the feeding ecology of gray whales be shaping the ecology of the Bay itself? The specific methodologies will be shaped collaboratively with the successful candidate based on their background and interests and the expertise of project principal investigators. These methods may include: ● benthic ecology surveys and sampling ● direct vessel-based observation of gray whale feeding ecology within the San Francisco Bay ● uncrewed aerial systems surveys ● review and analysis of long-term datasets curated by Bay monitoring and management agencies ● lab-based analyses of whale tissue ● prey energetic analyses ● molecular/eDNA diet analysis ● stable isotope analysis ● contaminant sampling ● acoustic benthic mapping ● ecological modeling The postdoc will work closely with collaborators at UC Santa Barbara, UC Berkeley, the Marine Mammal Center, and  additional stakeholders. The postdoc will take the lead in defining field and lab research plans, managing the logistics for accomplishment of this plan, collection and analysis of all data, publishing the results in peer-reviewed journal(s), and communicating about the research with relevant stakeholders. The field research work will be conducted primarily in the Bay Area during the gray whale field season (approximately Jan-May). However, the on-site work location for the remainder of the season will be UC Santa Barbara. Salary is competitive and commensurate with the applicant's qualifications and experience. Qualifications Basic qualifications (required at time of application) Applicants must have completed all requirements for a PhD program (or equivalent), except the dissertation, in biology, ecology, chemistry, environmental sciences, or a related field at the time of application. Additional qualifications (required at time of start) Applicants must have a doctoral degree in biology, ecology, chemistry, environmental sciences, or a related field at time of the appointment. Preferred qualifications Depending on the candidate’s area of expertise and anticipated research question(s), the ideal candidate may bring: ● hands-on experience with benthic invertebrate sampling, nutritional ecology, feeding ecology, or marine mammal ecology ● background in the toxicology of marine invertebrates or marine mammals, particularly contaminant transfer through benthic food webs ● experience with field/analytical methods such as stable isotopes, eDNA, UAV/drone-based data collection, or sonar sampling of the benthos ● statistical fluency (e.g., mixed effects models, Bayesian statistics) applied to noisy field biomonitoring data or long-term ecological datasets ● experience operating and/or crewing small research vessels for field sampling in contexts similar to San Francisco Bay ● or other relevant technical skills as described in the candidate’s application Additionally, the ideal candidate will: ● be willing to travel to field work sites, such as the San Francisco Bay, to lead field data collection activities during gray whale season ● adopt a rigorous and scholarly approach to their science, with a strong track record of presentations and peer-reviewed publications ● be a respectful, professional, and reliable team player who values creating and maintaining strong collegial bonds ● have excellent time management skills, accomplishing research goals by creating realistic but ambitious plans and initiating open communication with the PIs, colleagues, and stakeholders when plans must be modified. Application Requirements Document requirements Curriculum Vitae -   Your most recently updated C.V. Cover Letter Reference requirements 3 required (contact information only) The search committee will contact references of the top candidate(s) after the interview(s). After references are checked, a top candidate will be chosen. Apply link: https://recruit.ap.ucsb.edu/JPF03150 Help contact: lswanson@ucsb.edu About UC Santa Barbara As a condition of employment, the finalist will be required to disclose if they are subject to any final administrative or judicial decisions within the last seven years determining that they committed any misconduct. “Misconduct” means any violation of the policies or laws governing conduct at the applicant’s previous place of employment, including, but not limited to, violations of policies or laws prohibiting sexual harassment, sexual assault, or other forms of harassment or discrimination as defined by the employer. UC Sexual Violence and Sexual Harassment Policy UC Anti-Discrimination Policy for Employees, students and third parties APM - 035: Affirmative Action and Nondiscrimination in Employment Additionally, you will be required to comply with the University of California Policy on Vaccination Programs , as may be amended or revised from time to time.  Federal, state, or local public health directives may impose additional requirements. The University of California is an Equal Opportunity Employer. All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, national origin, disability, age, protected veteran status, or other protected status under state or federal law. Job location Santa Barbara, CA UCSB Apply now to Postdoctoral Researcher in Gray Whale Ecology and Biology, Marine Science Institute, UC Santa Barbara or Log in to your portfolio Need help? Contact the hiring department . The University of California Santa Barbara is an Equal Opportunity/Affirmative Action Employer. You have the right to an equal employment opportunity . The University of California Santa Barbara is committed to providing reasonable accommodations to applicants with disabilities . See our Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act Annual Security Reports Academic Personnel Recruit University of California Santa Barbara Accessibility About California Consumer Privacy Act (CCPA) Opt-Out Icon Privacy Policy © 2007-2026 The Regents of the University of California. All rights reserved."
    },
    "https://recruit.ap.ucsb.edu/JPF03156": {
      "title": "Screenwriting and/or Production Lecturer in Film and Media Studies (Pooled Recruitment) (JPF03156)",
      "description": "Screenwriting and/or Production Lecturer in Film and Media Studies (Pooled Recruitment) (JPF03156) - Recruit Skip to main content Recruit Home Open Recruitments Screenwriting and/or Production Lecturer in Film and Media Studies (Pooled Recruitment) (JPF03156) Screenwriting and/or Production Lecturer in Film and Media Studies (Pooled Recruitment) Apply now to Screenwriting and/or Production Lecturer in Film and Media Studies (Pooled Recruitment) Job #JPF03156 Film and Media Studies / College of Letters & Science - Humanities and Fine Arts / UC Santa Barbara Position overview Position title: Lecturer Salary range: See Table 15 for the salary range for this position.  The actual appointment percentage is variable, derived from an estimated full-time (100%) annual salary range of $73,817-78,314. Percent appointments may vary. Percent time: 33.33-100% Application Window Open date: August 26, 2026 Next review date: Wednesday, Sep 9, 2026 at 11:59pm (Pacific Time) Apply by this date to ensure full consideration by the committee. Final date: Wednesday, Mar 31, 2027 at 11:59pm (Pacific Time) Applications will continue to be accepted until this date, but those received after the review date will only be considered if the position has not yet been filled. Position description The Department of Film and Media Studies at UC Santa Barbara is seeking a lecturer to teach courses in production and screenwriting in the 2026-2027 academic year, with options including Fall, Winter, and Spring quarters, and potentially Summer 2027. Positions may range from 33% to 100% time. In Fall 2026, FAMST 112: Lighting is a workshop approach to explorations in how lighting affects the moving image. Students gain hands-on experience working with multiple lighting scenarios including fixtures, greenscreen and natural light. Class is conducted on a soundstage. It includes 2 lectures per week of 2 hours each. Screenwriting courses are workshop style courses, weighted towards creative practice. They give students a strong theoretical grounding in screen story and in screenwriting. Courses focus on storytelling and screenwriting skills, and on applying them creatively as a screenwriter. Specific topics are open for discussion. New specific iterations of screenwriting courses can be developed according to instructor interest. Applications are invited for anticipated academic teaching positions in Fall, Winter, and Spring quarters in the above-mentioned areas. Additional areas of hire may arise. This is a call to constitute a pool of candidates; actual positions become available depending on curricular needs and external candidates will only be considered as teaching needs arise. The posting will remain open until March 31, 2027 to accommodate department needs. Please note: The use of a lecturer pool does not guarantee that an open position exists at the time you are applying. See the review date specified in UC Recruit to learn whether the department is currently reviewing applications for an upcoming opening. If there is no future review date specified, your application may not be considered at this time. Department : https://www.filmandmedia.ucsb.edu/ Qualifications Basic qualifications (required at time of application) MFA  degree in Film, Television, or related Media Practices, or at least five years of professional experience Preferred qualifications Teaching experience at the college or university level for at least one year A demonstrated record of teaching excellence and effectiveness as evidenced by positive teaching evaluations Application Requirements Document requirements Curriculum Vitae -   Your most recently updated C.V. Cover Letter -   Your cover letter should include a discussion of qualifications and experience Statement of Teaching -   Describe your methodological and theoretical approach to pedagogy — how you teach, why you teach that way, and the intellectual frameworks that inform both. Description of Proposed Courses -   Describe your proposed production and/or screenwriting course(s) Sample Course Syllabus #1 -   Please upload proposed course syllabi fitting the courses described based on your own area of expertise. Sample Course Syllabus #2 (Optional) Sample Syllabus #3 (Optional) Teaching Evaluations (Optional) Reference requirements 3 required (contact information only) Please provide the names and complete contact information for 3 references. The department will conduct a reference check for the finalist before extending an offer. Apply link: https://recruit.ap.ucsb.edu/JPF03156 Help contact: savannah@filmandmedia.ucsb.edu About UC Santa Barbara As a condition of employment, the finalist will be required to disclose if they are subject to any final administrative or judicial decisions within the last seven years determining that they committed any misconduct. “Misconduct” means any violation of the policies or laws governing conduct at the applicant’s previous place of employment, including, but not limited to, violations of policies or laws prohibiting sexual harassment, sexual assault, or other forms of harassment or discrimination as defined by the employer. UC Sexual Violence and Sexual Harassment Policy UC Anti-Discrimination Policy for Employees, students and third parties APM - 035: Affirmative Action and Nondiscrimination in Employment Additionally, you will be required to comply with the University of California Policy on Vaccination Programs , as may be amended or revised from time to time.  Federal, state, or local public health directives may impose additional requirements. The University of California is an Equal Opportunity Employer. All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, national origin, disability, age, protected veteran status, or other protected status under state or federal law. Job location Santa Barbara, CA Apply now to Screenwriting and/or Production Lecturer in Film and Media Studies (Pooled Recruitment) or Log in to your portfolio Need help? Contact the hiring department . The University of California Santa Barbara is an Equal Opportunity/Affirmative Action Employer. You have the right to an equal employment opportunity . The University of California Santa Barbara is committed to providing reasonable accommodations to applicants with disabilities . See our Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act Annual Security Reports Academic Personnel Recruit University of California Santa Barbara Accessibility About California Consumer Privacy Act (CCPA) Opt-Out Icon Privacy Policy © 2007-2026 The Regents of the University of California. All rights reserved."
