    "https://recruit.ap.ucsb.edu/JPF03135": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor in Quantum / AMO Physics, Department of Physics (JPF03135)",
      "description": "Assistant Professor in Quantum / AMO Physics, Department of Physics (JPF03135) - Recruit Skip to main content Recruit Home Open Recruitments Assistant Professor in Quantum / AMO Physics, Department of Physics (JPF03135) Assistant Professor in Quantum / AMO Physics, Department of Physics Apply now to Assistant Professor in Quantum / AMO Physics, Department of Physics Job #JPF03135 Physics / College of Letters & Science - Mathematical, Life, and Physical Sciences / UC Santa Barbara Position overview Position title: Assistant Professor Salary range: A reasonable range for this position is $100,000 - $160,000. Percent time: 100% Anticipated start: July 1, 2027 or later Position duration: Tenure track Application Window Open date: September 16, 2026 Next review date: Wednesday, Nov 18, 2026 at 11:59pm (Pacific Time) Apply by this date to ensure full consideration by the committee. Final date: Wednesday, Jun 30, 2027 at 11:59pm (Pacific Time) Applications will continue to be accepted until this date, but those received after the review date will only be considered if the position has not yet been filled. Position description The Department of Physics at the University of California, Santa Barbara invites applications for a tenure-track faculty position at the level of Assistant Professor. The Department is seeking individuals working in quantum science, with an emphasis in atomic, molecular, and optical (AMO) physics. The University of California, Santa Barbara invites pioneering researchers to join its growing quantum research community as part of the Quantum Hiring Initiative. Building on a rich history of scientific breakthroughs - highlighted by the 2025 Nobel Prize in Physics - this initiative brings together faculty across disciplines to advance the full spectrum of quantum science, from synthetic materials and superconducting qubits to quantum topology. Candidates will join a supportive, interdisciplinary ecosystem backed by premier facilities, including the NSF Quantum Foundry, the Eddleman Quantum Institute, and the Nanofabrication Facility, alongside strong connections to industry and national quantum initiatives. To learn more about our existing faculty and research strengths, visit UCSB Quantum: Driving the Future of Quantum Science . For details and to apply for these individual recruitments, please visit: Quantum Faculty Positions in Mathematics, Chemistry and Physics | UCSB . Information about the Department and its research activities may be found at Home | Department of Physics | UC Santa Barbara . Experimentalists in the Department benefit from close connections with programs across campus, including at the Kavli Institute for Theoretical Physics and the California NanoSystems Institute. Responsibilities of faculty members generally include the development of an actively funded research program of high quality, teaching at undergraduate and/or graduate levels, mentorship of undergraduate students, recruitment, supervision, and mentorship of graduate students, and participation in university service and professional activities that meet expectations for faculty rank and step. UCSB has a strong tradition of collaborative research, and successful candidates will be able to leverage synergies across the campus as appropriate. Qualifications Basic qualifications (required at time of application) Applicants must have completed all requirements for a PhD (or equivalent) in Physics or a related field, except the dissertation, at the time of application. Additional qualifications (required at time of start) PhD in Physics or a related field is required by appointment start date. Preferred qualifications •  Research in quantum science with emphasis in atomic, molecular, and optical (AMO) physics. •  A demonstrated record of excellence in research, as evidenced by high-quality publications and impact on the field. •  Record of, or potential for, outstanding teaching. Application Requirements Document requirements Curriculum Vitae -   Please submit a complete CV including full publication, awards, and mentorship records. Cover Letter -   To facilitate efficient review of applications, on a single page with reasonable formatting, please concisely introduce us to your research and highlight your unique qualifications. Statement of Research -   Discuss up to three of your most important contributions to science, your current research focus, your plans for the future, and the potential for sustained impact of your proposed research. This statement may also include information about possible areas of collaboration within the department or campus. Please try to keep this statement to at most 4 pages. Statement of Teaching -   The search committee will use this document to evaluate your potential as an instructor. Please provide a statement that includes teaching and mentorship experiences to date, a brief summary of your teaching philosophy, and one or more topics that you would be interested in teaching (~2 pages). Reference requirements 3-5 letters of reference required Applicants should provide the names and contact information of at least 3 references and must request letters of references within the UC Recruit system in order to complete application requirements. Apply link: https://recruit.ap.ucsb.edu/JPF03135 Help contact: dill@physics.ucsb.edu About UC Santa Barbara As a condition of employment, the finalist will be required to disclose if they are subject to any final administrative or judicial decisions within the last seven years determining that they committed any misconduct. “Misconduct” means any violation of the policies or laws governing conduct at the applicant’s previous place of employment, including, but not limited to, violations of policies or laws prohibiting sexual harassment, sexual assault, or other forms of harassment or discrimination as defined by the employer. UC Sexual Violence and Sexual Harassment Policy UC Anti-Discrimination Policy for Employees, students and third parties APM - 035: Affirmative Action and Nondiscrimination in Employment Additionally, you will be required to comply with the University of California Policy on Vaccination Programs , as may be amended or revised from time to time.  Federal, state, or local public health directives may impose additional requirements. The University of California is an Equal Opportunity Employer. All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, national origin, disability, age, protected veteran status, or other protected status under state or federal law. Job location Santa Barbara, CA Apply now to Assistant Professor in Quantum / AMO Physics, Department of Physics or Log in to your portfolio Need help? Contact the hiring department . The University of California Santa Barbara is an Equal Opportunity/Affirmative Action Employer. You have the right to an equal employment opportunity . The University of California Santa Barbara is committed to providing reasonable accommodations to applicants with disabilities . See our Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act Annual Security Reports Academic Personnel Recruit University of California Santa Barbara Accessibility About California Consumer Privacy Act (CCPA) Opt-Out Icon Privacy Policy © 2007-2026 The Regents of the University of California. All rights reserved."
    },
    "https://recruit.ap.ucsb.edu/JPF03152": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor in Experimental Quantum Physical Chemistry (JPF03152)",
      "description": "Assistant Professor in Experimental Quantum Physical Chemistry (JPF03152) - Recruit Skip to main content Recruit Home Open Recruitments Assistant Professor in Experimental Quantum Physical Chemistry (JPF03152) Assistant Professor in Experimental Quantum Physical Chemistry Apply now to Assistant Professor in Experimental Quantum Physical Chemistry Job #JPF03152 Chemistry and Biochemistry / College of Letters & Science - Mathematical, Life, and Physical Sciences / UC Santa Barbara Position overview Position title: Assistant Professor in Experimental Quantum Physical Chemistry Salary range: A reasonable estimate for this position is $100,000 to $160,000 Percent time: 100% Time, Academic Year Anticipated start: July 1, 2027, or later Application Window Open date: September 16, 2026 Next review date: Friday, Oct 16, 2026 at 11:59pm (Pacific Time) Apply by this date to ensure full consideration by the committee. Final date: Wednesday, Mar 31, 2027 at 11:59pm (Pacific Time) Applications will continue to be accepted until this date, but those received after the review date will only be considered if the position has not yet been filled. Position description The Department of Chemistry and Biochemistry at the University of California, Santa Barbara invites applications for a tenure-track faculty position at the level of Assistant Professor with a research focus in Experimental Quantum Physical Chemistry.  This is part of a Quantum Cluster hire. The Department seeks an experimentalist who develops and applies advanced spectroscopic approaches, including ultrafast and multidimensional methods, to understand and control quantum states, their dynamics, and their interactions in chemical systems, both static and driven. The search is broadly defined across molecular, supramolecular, nanoscale, and solid-state platforms. The successful candidate's independent research program, while anchored in chemistry, will ideally connect the synthesis of quantum material platforms, theory and computation, and quantitative characterization efforts on campus. The successful candidate will establish a creative, externally funded research program, teach at the undergraduate and graduate levels, including in physical chemistry and related areas, recruit and mentor students and researchers, and contribute to departmental and university service. The Department particularly values a collaborative research program that leverages synergies within the Department of Chemistry and Biochemistry, across the Physics, Materials, and Chemical Engineering Departments, and with relevant institutes and facilities. The University of California, Santa Barbara invites pioneering researchers to join its growing quantum research community. Building on a rich history of scientific breakthroughs - highlighted by the 2025 Nobel Prize in Physics - this initiative brings together faculty across disciplines to advance the full spectrum of quantum science, from synthetic materials and superconducting qubits to quantum topology. Candidates will join a supportive, interdisciplinary ecosystem backed by premier facilities, including the NSF Quantum Foundry, the Eddleman Quantum Institute, and the Nanofabrication Facility, alongside strong connections to industry and national quantum initiatives. Division : https://science.ucsb.edu/quantum/faculty-opportunities Unit : https://chem.ucsb.edu/ Qualifications Basic qualifications (required at time of application) Applicants must have completed all requirements for a PhD (or equivalent), except the dissertation, in Chemistry, Chemical Physics, Physics, Materials Science, or a closely related field by the time of application. Additional qualifications (required at time of start) The PhD (or equivalent) must be conferred by the anticipated appointment start date. Preferred qualifications Postdoctoral or equivalent advanced research experience. A demonstrated record of research excellence and the potential to establish an original, independent, externally funded, and high-impact research program in experimental quantum physical chemistry. Experience with advanced spectroscopic or related measurement methods for probing or controlling quantum states and dynamics. Research interests involving ultrafast or multidimensional spectroscopy, driven quantum systems, or emergent quantum phenomena in molecular, supramolecular, nanoscale, or solid-state chemical systems. A clear chemistry-centered research vision that complements UCSB’s existing strengths and supports collaboration across the quantum cluster. A record of, or strong potential for, excellence in teaching and mentoring. Application Requirements Document requirements Curriculum Vitae -   Please submit a full CV Cover Letter -   (~2 pages) Statement of Research -   Discuss up to three of your most important contributions to science, your current research focus, your plans for the future, and the potential for sustained impact of your proposed research. This statement may also include information about possible areas of collaboration within the department or campus. (~3 pages) Statement of Teaching and Mentoring -   Please provide a statement that includes teaching and mentorship experiences to date, a brief summary of your teaching philosophy, and one or more topics that you would be interested in teaching. (~ 2 pages) Representative Publication #1 -   Please provide a pdf of the publication. Representative Publication #2 -   Please provide a pdf of the publication. Representative Publication #3 -   Please provide a pdf of the publication. Reference requirements 3-5 letters of reference required Provide the names and contact information for at least 3 references. Request letters of reference within the system in order to complete the application requirement. Apply link: https://recruit.ap.ucsb.edu/JPF03152 Help contact: nmccoy@ucsb.edu About UC Santa Barbara As a condition of employment, the finalist will be required to disclose if they are subject to any final administrative or judicial decisions within the last seven years determining that they committed any misconduct. “Misconduct” means any violation of the policies or laws governing conduct at the applicant’s previous place of employment, including, but not limited to, violations of policies or laws prohibiting sexual harassment, sexual assault, or other forms of harassment or discrimination as defined by the employer. UC Sexual Violence and Sexual Harassment Policy UC Anti-Discrimination Policy for Employees, students and third parties APM - 035: Affirmative Action and Nondiscrimination in Employment Additionally, you will be required to comply with the University of California Policy on Vaccination Programs , as may be amended or revised from time to time.  Federal, state, or local public health directives may impose additional requirements. The University of California is an Equal Opportunity Employer. All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, national origin, disability, age, protected veteran status, or other protected status under state or federal law. Job location Department of Chemistry and Biochemistry, University of California, Santa Barbara, Building 232, Santa Barbara, CA 93106-9510 Apply now to Assistant Professor in Experimental Quantum Physical Chemistry or Log in to your portfolio Need help? Contact the hiring department . The University of California Santa Barbara is an Equal Opportunity/Affirmative Action Employer. You have the right to an equal employment opportunity . The University of California Santa Barbara is committed to providing reasonable accommodations to applicants with disabilities . See our Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act Annual Security Reports Academic Personnel Recruit University of California Santa Barbara Accessibility About California Consumer Privacy Act (CCPA) Opt-Out Icon Privacy Policy © 2007-2026 The Regents of the University of California. All rights reserved."
    },
    "https://recruit.ap.ucsb.edu/JPF03153": {
      "title": "Faculty Position: Quantum Topology & Quantum Computing (JPF03153)",
      "description": "Faculty Position: Quantum Topology & Quantum Computing (JPF03153) - Recruit Skip to main content Recruit Home Open Recruitments Faculty Position: Quantum Topology & Quantum Computing (JPF03153) Faculty Position: Quantum Topology & Quantum Computing Apply now to Faculty Position: Quantum Topology & Quantum Computing Job #JPF03153 Mathematics / College of Letters & Science - Mathematical, Life, and Physical Sciences / UC Santa Barbara Position overview Position title: Assistant Professor Salary range: A reasonable estimate that the University expects to pay for this position at 100% time is $100,000 - $160,000 Percent time: 100 Anticipated start: July 1 , 2027 or after Application Window Open date: September 16, 2026 Next review date: Friday, Oct 23, 2026 at 11:59pm (Pacific Time) Apply by this date to ensure full consideration by the committee. Final date: Wednesday, Jun 30, 2027 at 11:59pm (Pacific Time) Applications will continue to be accepted until this date, but those received after the review date will only be considered if the position has not yet been filled. Position description The University of California, Santa Barbara announces a strategic expansion of its world-leading quantum research enterprise, building on an unrivaled legacy of excellence recently punctuated by the 2025 Nobel Prize in Physics. This initiative reinforces an extraordinary community of faculty whose work spans the full quantum spectrum—from superconducting qubits and synthetic materials to the theoretical foundations of quantum topology. UCSB leverages a premier research infrastructure including the NSF Quantum Foundry, the Eddleman Quantum Institute, and a state-of-the-art Nanofabrication Facility. These resources, combined with deep on-campus partnerships with global technology giants, position the university at the forefront of the federal government's \"big bets\" in quantum information science. To further strengthen this elite group, recruitment is underway for three foundational faculty positions in the first phase of this strategic cluster hire: an Atomic, Molecular, and Optical (AMO) Experimentalist in Physics, JPF03135 ; a specialist in Quantum Topology in Mathematics ; and an Experimental Physical Chemist in Chemistry and Biochemistry, JPF03152 Faculty Position: Quantum Topology & Quantum Computing As a vital part of this Quantum Cluster, the Department of Mathematics at the University of California, Santa Barbara invites applications for a tenure track  faculty position at the level of Assistant  Professor, with an anticipated start date of July 1, 2027. The Department is looking for accomplished mathematicians with a particular emphasis in the area of QuantumTopology broadly construed, and its intersections with quantum computing. Preference will be given to candidates with strong potential to collaborate with researchers in other disciplines, especially physics or computer science, as well as to interact robustly with colleagues specializing in Topology. Responsibilities of faculty members generally include the development of an actively funded research program of high quality, teaching at undergraduate and/or graduate levels, mentorship of undergraduate students, recruitment, supervision, and mentorship of graduate students, and  participation in university service and professional activities that meets expectations for faculty rank and step. UCSB has a strong tradition of collaborative research, and successful candidates will be able to leverage synergies across the campus as appropriate to their field. Our Research Environment UC Santa Barbara offers a unique environment where innovative, interdisciplinary, and foundational research is conducted in a collegial atmosphere. We are looking for candidates who have demonstrated exceptional promise through novel research. Qualifications Basic qualifications (required at time of application) At a minimum, applicants must have completed all requirements for a PhD (or equivalent) except the dissertation (or equivalent) in mathematics or a related field at the time of application. Additional qualifications (required at time of start) Applicants must have a PhD degree or equivalent in mathematics or a related field by anticipated appointment start date (July 1, 2027). Preferred qualifications A demonstrated record of excellence in research, and a record of (or potential for) outstanding teaching are desired. Successful candidates will have a record of collaborative research, and will be able to leverage synergies within the Mathematics Department and Division. Application Requirements Document requirements Curriculum Vitae -   Your most recently updated C.V. AMS Cover Sheet -   available online at: AMS.org Statement of Research -   Describe research experience and interests, highlighting major contributions to the field thus far, as well as areas to be investigated in future work. This statement may also include information about potential areas of collaboration within the department or campus and the research’s potential for sustained impact. (3-8 pages) Statement of Teaching Philosophy -   Describe teaching experience and interests, highlighting courses taught and/or planned and teaching approaches and practices (e.g., lecturing style, assessment choices) adopted or likely to be adopted in these courses. This statement may also include a description of mentoring experience and interests. (1-3 pages) Reference requirements 4-5 letters of reference required Applicants will need to arrange for four (4) letters of reference to be submitted electronically to the UC Recruit website. It is encouraged that one of the letters will be directed towards teaching.  Applicants are responsible for soliciting letters within the system. Apply link: https://recruit.ap.ucsb.edu/JPF03153 Help contact: ddickinson@math.ucsb.edu About UC Santa Barbara As a condition of employment, the finalist will be required to disclose if they are subject to any final administrative or judicial decisions within the last seven years determining that they committed any misconduct. “Misconduct” means any violation of the policies or laws governing conduct at the applicant’s previous place of employment, including, but not limited to, violations of policies or laws prohibiting sexual harassment, sexual assault, or other forms of harassment or discrimination as defined by the employer. UC Sexual Violence and Sexual Harassment Policy UC Anti-Discrimination Policy for Employees, students and third parties APM - 035: Affirmative Action and Nondiscrimination in Employment Additionally, you will be required to comply with the University of California Policy on Vaccination Programs , as may be amended or revised from time to time.  Federal, state, or local public health directives may impose additional requirements. The University of California is an Equal Opportunity Employer. All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, national origin, disability, age, protected veteran status, or other protected status under state or federal law. Job location Santa Barbara, CA Apply now to Faculty Position: Quantum Topology & Quantum Computing or Log in to your portfolio Need help? Contact the hiring department . The University of California Santa Barbara is an Equal Opportunity/Affirmative Action Employer. You have the right to an equal employment opportunity . The University of California Santa Barbara is committed to providing reasonable accommodations to applicants with disabilities . See our Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act Annual Security Reports Academic Personnel Recruit University of California Santa Barbara Accessibility About California Consumer Privacy Act (CCPA) Opt-Out Icon Privacy Policy © 2007-2026 The Regents of the University of California. All rights reserved."
    },
    "https://recruit.ap.ucsb.edu/JPF03165": {
      "title": "COAST PCL Robotics Project Scientist (California NanoSystems Institute) (JPF03165)",
      "description": "COAST PCL Robotics Project Scientist (California NanoSystems Institute) (JPF03165) - Recruit Skip to main content Recruit Home Open Recruitments COAST PCL Robotics Project Scientist (California NanoSystems Institute) (JPF03165) COAST PCL Robotics Project Scientist (California NanoSystems Institute) Apply now to COAST PCL Robotics Project Scientist (California NanoSystems Institute) Job #JPF03165 California NanoSystems Institute / Office of Research / UC Santa Barbara Position overview Position title: COAST PCL Robotics Project Scientist Salary range: A reasonable salary range that the University expects to pay for this position at 100% time is $94,400 to $138,300. Percent time: 100% Anticipated start: November 1, 2026 or later. Position duration: 1 year, 100% appointment with possibility of renewal on an annual basis. Application Window Open date: September 17, 2026 Next review date: Friday, Oct 2, 2026 at 11:59pm (Pacific Time) Apply by this date to ensure full consideration by the committee. Final date: Wednesday, Mar 31, 2027 at 11:59pm (Pacific Time) Applications will continue to be accepted until this date, but those received after the review date will only be considered if the position has not yet been filled. Position description The Computation-Optimized Automated Soft materials Technology Programmable Cloud Laboratory Node (COAST PCL), funded by the National Science Foundation, is a national AI-driven materials innovation infrastructure that combines autonomous robotics, advanced scientific instrumentation, and AI-guided experiment planning. This research ecosystem connects the broad and disparate communities of Materials, Chemistry, Engineering, and Computer Science, and enables rapid discovery and manufacturing of materials with highly controlled and programmable composition, properties, and functionality. COAST PCL is seeking a motivated individual to grow a world-class resource for AI-guided autonomous experimentation and to take a lead role in the development and application of these next-generation methods in advancing COAST PCL's research mission and enabling a national user base to utilize this resource. Reporting to the Associate Director at UCSB, the Robotics Project Scientist will lead the robotic manipulation and laboratory automation capability at COAST PCL. The Robotics Project Scientist will spend 30% time, in tandem with the in-house research team, on making significant and creative contributions to in-house research, including designing, developing, and deploying robotic manipulation systems including robot arms, end effectors, and dexterous hands, for automated synthesis and characterization tasks, applying both learning-based and classical manipulation techniques to handle the variability of real laboratory materials and operations, and developing human-robot interaction approaches that allow researchers and technicians to work alongside and direct robotic systems safely and intuitively. External users will also consult with the Robotics Project Scientist to develop a Project Feasibility and Research plan. The Robotics Project Scientist will review topically relevant COAST PCL user proposals and advise on whether the proposed work can be executed on the facility's robotic platforms, or if a proposal requires revision to be accepted, translating scientific requirements into robotic capabilities. The Robotics Project Scientist can also advise on how to set up the work to maximize the likelihood of success. The Robotics Project Scientist will devote 70% of their time to serve as a scientific and technical expert and train internal and external users in the operation of these systems, and on setting up, carrying out, and troubleshooting automated experiments in support of their work at COAST PCL. The Robotics Project Scientist will run a training module as part of the COAST PCL Summer School and other similar workshops that will serve to highlight the facilities and research capabilities and promote use by internal and external users. Because COAST PCL is accessed remotely by a national user base, the Robotics Project Scientist will be responsible for making robust automated workflows. This includes designing the robotic workflows for experiments submitted by outside users on the platform. The Robotics Project Scientist will be responsible for troubleshooting, maintaining, calibrating, and documenting robotic systems for day-to-day operations. In addition, they will be required to collaborate across disciplines to bridge robotics with chemistry, materials, and software teams, translating scientific requirements into robotic capabilities. Above all, COAST PCL seeks an individual who is passionate about contributing to cutting-edge scientific research. This is a hands-on technical role for someone who is current with the state of the field and excited to apply it to scientific automation. Qualifications Basic qualifications (required at time of application) • Ph.D. in Robotics, or a Ph.D. in a closely related engineering field (Mechanical Engineering, Electrical Engineering, Computer Science) with demonstrated depth in robotics, at the time of application. Preferred qualifications • Strong understanding of recent advances in the field, particularly in manipulation and learning-based methods. • Demonstrated experience in robotic manipulation, including work with robot arms and end effectors or robotic hands. • Strong programming skills, with proficiency in Python. • Experience with human-robot interaction. • Familiarity with robotics frameworks. • Experience deploying robotic systems in scientific, laboratory, and/or industrial automation settings. • Familiarity with materials, chemistry, and/or polymer workflows. • Experience integrating robotics with analytical instrumentation or high-throughput experimentation. • Experience with unattended or remotely operated systems, including error detection, failure recovery, and logging. • Experience with mechanical design and fabrication of fixtures, tooling, or custom end effectors, including CAD. • Familiarity with machine safety practices and standards for industrial and collaborative robots, and with safe handling of chemicals in an automated environment. • Track record of peer-reviewed publications and/or deployed robotic systems. • Ability to work collaboratively in a team-oriented work environment. • Ability to work creatively and independently and follow through on assignments with minimal or no direction. • Excellent interpersonal and communication skills to interact with faculty, staff, students, visitors, and the public. • Skilled in project management, prioritizing workload, assessing timelines, and coordinating numerous assignments/projects simultaneously under heavy workload. • Ability to utilize a variety of communication methods to move projects forward in a timely manner. • Ability to work nights and weekends as needed. Application Requirements Document requirements Curriculum Vitae -   Your most recently updated C.V. Cover Letter Statement of Research Reference requirements 3-6 required (contact information only) Applicants must provide the names and contact information (email and telephone) of at least 3 references.  References of the top candidates will be contacted.  Candidates under serious consideration may be required to provide additional references. Apply link: https://recruit.ap.ucsb.edu/JPF03165 Help contact: debbie@cnsi.ucsb.edu About UC Santa Barbara As a condition of employment, the finalist will be required to disclose if they are subject to any final administrative or judicial decisions within the last seven years determining that they committed any misconduct. “Misconduct” means any violation of the policies or laws governing conduct at the applicant’s previous place of employment, including, but not limited to, violations of policies or laws prohibiting sexual harassment, sexual assault, or other forms of harassment or discrimination as defined by the employer. UC Sexual Violence and Sexual Harassment Policy UC Anti-Discrimination Policy for Employees, students and third parties APM - 035: Affirmative Action and Nondiscrimination in Employment Additionally, you will be required to comply with the University of California Policy on Vaccination Programs , as may be amended or revised from time to time.  Federal, state, or local public health directives may impose additional requirements. The University of California is an Equal Opportunity Employer. All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, national origin, disability, age, protected veteran status, or other protected status under state or federal law. Job location Goleta, California (UCSB OASIS) Apply now to COAST PCL Robotics Project Scientist (California NanoSystems Institute) or Log in to your portfolio Need help? Contact the hiring department . The University of California Santa Barbara is an Equal Opportunity/Affirmative Action Employer. You have the right to an equal employment opportunity . The University of California Santa Barbara is committed to providing reasonable accommodations to applicants with disabilities . See our Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act Annual Security Reports Academic Personnel Recruit University of California Santa Barbara Accessibility About California Consumer Privacy Act (CCPA) Opt-Out Icon Privacy Policy © 2007-2026 The Regents of the University of California. All rights reserved."
    },
    "https://recruit.ap.ucsb.edu/JPF03137": {
      "title": "Professor in Social Consequences of AI in Organizational Communication (JPF03137)",
      "description": "Professor in Social Consequences of AI in Organizational Communication (JPF03137) - Recruit Skip to main content Recruit Home Open Recruitments Professor in Social Consequences of AI in Organizational Communication (JPF03137) Professor in Social Consequences of AI in Organizational Communication Apply now to Professor in Social Consequences of AI in Organizational Communication Job #JPF03137 Communication / College of Letters & Science - Social Sciences / UC Santa Barbara Position overview Position title: Assistant, Associate, or Professor Salary range: A reasonable estimate that the University expects to pay for this position at 100% time is $95,000 - $435,000  at the Assistant, Associate, or Professor Level. Percent time: 100% Anticipated start: July 1, 2027 or later Application Window Open date: September 21, 2026 Next review date: Wednesday, Oct 21, 2026 at 11:59pm (Pacific Time) Apply by this date to ensure full consideration by the committee. Final date: Wednesday, Mar 31, 2027 at 11:59pm (Pacific Time) Applications will continue to be accepted until this date, but those received after the review date will only be considered if the position has not yet been filled. Position description The UCSB Department of Communication is recruiting for a tenure-track/tenured open rank faculty position. Artificial intelligence (AI) is fundamentally transforming how humans communicate, coordinate, and organize across workplaces, institutions, and society. AI increasingly shapes organizational decision-making, collaboration, knowledge production, expertise, and identity while reconfiguring power, authority, accountability, and the boundaries between human and machine agency. These developments create new opportunities to revisit foundational organizational communication theories while also demanding new theoretical and empirical approaches to understanding AI as a sociotechnical actor embedded within organizational and institutional systems. We seek a scholar who conducts innovative, theoretically grounded, and empirically rigorous research using quantitative, qualitative, or mixed-method approaches that addresses the social consequences of AI from an organizational communication perspective. This could include, but is not limited to, topics such as human-AI collaboration; algorithmic decision-making and governance; sustainability; visibility and surveillance; ethics and corporate social responsibility; power and inequality; and (dis)empowerment of underrepresented groups. We are particularly interested in scholarship that advances organizational communication theory by examining how algorithms or algorithmic systems reshape communication processes, organizing practices, and institutional change, rather than only treating AI as a methodological or analytical tool. We anticipate that the faculty hire will make meaningful connections and engage in multidisciplinary endeavors across campus such as the Mellichamp Initiative in Mind & Machine Intelligence, the Department of Technology Management, the Center for Information Technology & Society’s research foci in AI, the Pahl Center for the Study of Critical Social Issues, and campus-wide initiatives in AI and related communities of practice. UCSB is ranked among the top public universities in the United States, and the Department of Communication is also highly ranked in worldwide comparisons. UCSB is a designated Hispanic Serving Institution, and an Asian American and Native American Pacific Islander Serving Institution. It offers several programs to support incoming faculty, including housing assistance ( Faculty Housing | Campus Housing ) and various grant support programs. Qualifications Basic qualifications (required at time of application) Completed all requirements for a Ph.D. (or equivalent) in a Communication or related field except dissertation by the time of application. Additional qualifications (required at time of start) A Ph.D. (or equivalent) in communication or a related field by the time of appointment, with a desired start date of July 1, 2027 or later. Preferred qualifications Demonstrated research excellence relevant to the job call in the form of research quality, productivity, and a unique scholarly contribution to the area of the social consequences of AI in organizational communication as well as to the communication discipline. Potential or demonstrated ability to contribute to the department’s organizational communication area. Potential or demonstrated ability to attract and supervise graduate students interested in organizational communication. Potential or demonstrated ability to teach organizational communication and potentially other coursework to undergraduate and graduate students. Potential or demonstrated commitments to student success and inclusive pedagogy. Potential or demonstrated ability to be a conscientious member of the department and university community member. Application Requirements Document requirements Cover Letter -   A cover letter that highlights your qualifications, research agenda, teaching capabilities, and service contributions Curriculum Vitae -   Your most recently updated C.V. Evidence of Teaching Effectiveness -   Please upload one (or more) student evaluations from a course you have instructed or assisted. Teaching Statement -   Please upload a Teaching Statement which explains your teaching methodology. Research Sample #1 -   Published or completed research. Research Sample #2 -   Published or completed research. Research Sample #3 -   Published or completed research. Reference requirements 3-5 letters of reference required Applicants should provide the names and contact information for at least 3 references and must request letters of reference within the system in order to complete application requirements. Apply link: https://recruit.ap.ucsb.edu/JPF03137 Help contact: hyoder@ucsb.edu About UC Santa Barbara As a condition of employment, the finalist will be required to disclose if they are subject to any final administrative or judicial decisions within the last seven years determining that they committed any misconduct. “Misconduct” means any violation of the policies or laws governing conduct at the applicant’s previous place of employment, including, but not limited to, violations of policies or laws prohibiting sexual harassment, sexual assault, or other forms of harassment or discrimination as defined by the employer. UC Sexual Violence and Sexual Harassment Policy UC Anti-Discrimination Policy for Employees, students and third parties APM - 035: Affirmative Action and Nondiscrimination in Employment Additionally, you will be required to comply with the University of California Policy on Vaccination Programs , as may be amended or revised from time to time.  Federal, state, or local public health directives may impose additional requirements. The University of California is an Equal Opportunity Employer. All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, national origin, disability, age, protected veteran status, or other protected status under state or federal law. Job location Santa Barbara, CA Apply now to Professor in Social Consequences of AI in Organizational Communication or Log in to your portfolio Need help? Contact the hiring department . The University of California Santa Barbara is an Equal Opportunity/Affirmative Action Employer. You have the right to an equal employment opportunity . The University of California Santa Barbara is committed to providing reasonable accommodations to applicants with disabilities . See our Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act Annual Security Reports Academic Personnel Recruit University of California Santa Barbara Accessibility About California Consumer Privacy Act (CCPA) Opt-Out Icon Privacy Policy © 2007-2026 The Regents of the University of California. All rights reserved."
    },
    "https://recruit.ap.ucsb.edu/JPF03164": {
      "title": "Brain Imaging Center Director, Department of Psychological & Brain Sciences (JPF03164)",
      "description": "Brain Imaging Center Director, Department of Psychological & Brain Sciences (JPF03164) - Recruit Skip to main content Recruit Home Open Recruitments Brain Imaging Center Director, Department of Psychological & Brain Sciences (JPF03164) Brain Imaging Center Director, Department of Psychological & Brain Sciences Apply now to Brain Imaging Center Director, Department of Psychological & Brain Sciences Job #JPF03164 Psychological and Brain Sciences / College of Letters & Science - Mathematical, Life, and Physical Sciences / UC Santa Barbara Position overview Position title: Assistant Professor, Associate Professor , Full Professor Salary range: A reasonable estimate for this position is $130,000 to $400,000. Applicants that currently hold a senate faculty position at another UC campus should be aware of the policies governing intercampus faculty hiring & transfers. These policies can be found at UCOP APM 510-18. Percent time: 100% Anticipated start: July 1, 2027 or later Application Window Open date: September 21, 2026 Next review date: Sunday, Nov 1, 2026 at 11:59pm (Pacific Time) Apply by this date to ensure full consideration by the committee. Final date: Wednesday, Jun 30, 2027 at 11:59pm (Pacific Time) Applications will continue to be accepted until this date, but those received after the review date will only be considered if the position has not yet been filled. Position description The Department of Psychological & Brain Sciences at the University of California, Santa Barbara invites applications for an open-rank, tenured or tenure-track faculty position in Human Neuroscience (or a related field) to serve as the Director of the UCSB Brain Imaging Center (BIC). The ideal candidate will bring an active, cutting-edge research program in any domain of human neuroscience along with technical expertise, leadership skills, and a compelling vision for advancing the Center's core research mission. As Director of the BIC, the successful candidate will provide strategic and operational leadership for a state-of-the-art campus core facility. Equipped with a 3T Siemens PRISMAfit MRI system, MRI-compatible high-density EEG hardware, and advanced stimulus presentation devices, the Center supports cutting-edge structural and functional brain imaging research across campus. Directorship responsibilities include identifying growth opportunities for the BIC to advance a vibrant and innovative research program, securing external funding, managing administrative staff, and overseeing technical equipment maintenance and upgrades. The Director will also govern scanner access policies, handle annual budgeting and reporting, coordinate protocols for incidental findings, and actively support the broader UCSB neuroimaging community. In addition to leading the BIC, the candidate will contribute fully to the academic life of the Department of Psychological & Brain Sciences. Responsibilities of faculty members generally include the development of an actively funded research program of high quality, teaching at undergraduate and/or graduate levels, mentorship of undergraduate students, recruitment, supervision, and mentorship of graduate students, and participation in university service and professional activities that meets expectations for faculty rank and step. UCSB fosters a highly collaborative, interdisciplinary environment that connects brain sciences with bioengineering, computer science, biological sciences, and linguistics. Department : https://psych.ucsb.edu/ Lab : https://bic.ucsb.edu/ Qualifications Basic qualifications (required at time of application) A Ph.D.or  M.D., or equivalent degree conferred in Psychological and Brain Sciences or a discipline related to use of neuroimaging technologies Additional qualifications (required at time of start) ▪ 4 years of experience in a Tenure Track position (Assistant Prof. or equivalent) OR present appointment as an Associate Professor or higher ▪ Minimum of 4 years using MRI brain imagining technology . Preferred qualifications ● Minimum of 5 years of direct experience with MRI brain imaging methodologies. ● An active, cutting-edge research program aligned or synergistic with one or more core departmental areas (Cognition, Perception, & Cognitive Neuroscience; Neurobiology & Behavior; Social Psychology; or Developmental & Evolutionary Psychology). ● Record of scholarly publications using brain imaging techniques and/or analyzing brain imaging data. ● Previous leadership experience within a department, university, or core facility. ● Experience with technical oversight of brain imaging equipment and infrastructure. ● Experience in a clinical environment using brain imaging technology/(f)MRI ● Experience teaching (f)MRI methods at the undergraduate and/or graduate level. Application Requirements Document requirements Curriculum Vitae -   Your most recently updated C.V. Cover Letter -   Cover letters should include a brief summary of educational qualifications, research interests and how they align with the department's focus, notable publications, grants, and projects. Statement of Research -   Please describe research experience and interests, highlighting major contributions to the field thus far as well as areas to be investigated in future work. This statement may also include information about the candidate’s open science practices, possible areas of collaboration within the department or campus, and the potential for sustained impact of the research (3 pages max) Statement of Teaching & Mentoring -   Please describe teaching experience and interests, highlighting courses taught and/or planned, and teaching approaches and practices (e.g., lecturing style, assessment choices) adopted or likely to be adopted in these courses. This statement should also include a description of mentoring experience and approach (3 pages max) Statement of Experience with Brain Imaging Technology and Future Vision of the Brain Imaging Center -   Please describe training background, experience with, and approach to solving technical issues associated with modern MRI systems. If the candidate has played a key role in newly-developed methods or approaches, these should be highlighted. If there is prior experience in a leadership role supporting an imaging center, this should be described. This statement should articulate a vision for the future of UCSB's Brain Imaging Center in terms of research collaborations, funding, and innovations in brain imaging technology. (4 pages max) Representative Publication #1 -   Uploaded publications are encouraged to demonstrate scholarly accomplishments related to neuroimaging and/or creative or novel uses of neuroimaging to advance understanding of the human mind and brain Representative Publication #2 Representative Publication #3 Reference requirements 3 required (contact information only) Applicants should provide the names and contact information for 3 references and reference letters will be requested at the shortlist stage. Reference letters should address the candidate's potential as the BIC Director. Apply link: https://recruit.ap.ucsb.edu/JPF03164 Help contact: pamelawilks@ucsb.edu About UC Santa Barbara As a condition of employment, the finalist will be required to disclose if they are subject to any final administrative or judicial decisions within the last seven years determining that they committed any misconduct. “Misconduct” means any violation of the policies or laws governing conduct at the applicant’s previous place of employment, including, but not limited to, violations of policies or laws prohibiting sexual harassment, sexual assault, or other forms of harassment or discrimination as defined by the employer. UC Sexual Violence and Sexual Harassment Policy UC Anti-Discrimination Policy for Employees, students and third parties APM - 035: Affirmative Action and Nondiscrimination in Employment Additionally, you will be required to comply with the University of California Policy on Vaccination Programs , as may be amended or revised from time to time.  Federal, state, or local public health directives may impose additional requirements. The University of California is an Equal Opportunity Employer. All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, national origin, disability, age, protected veteran status, or other protected status under state or federal law. Job location Santa Barbara, CA Apply now to Brain Imaging Center Director, Department of Psychological & Brain Sciences or Log in to your portfolio Need help? Contact the hiring department . The University of California Santa Barbara is an Equal Opportunity/Affirmative Action Employer. You have the right to an equal employment opportunity . The University of California Santa Barbara is committed to providing reasonable accommodations to applicants with disabilities . See our Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act Annual Security Reports Academic Personnel Recruit University of California Santa Barbara Accessibility About California Consumer Privacy Act (CCPA) Opt-Out Icon Privacy Policy © 2007-2026 The Regents of the University of California. All rights reserved."
    },
    "https://recruit.ap.ucsb.edu/JPF03166": {
      "title": "Education and Science Engagement Coordinator (Academic Coordinator I)",
      "description": "Education and Science Engagement Coordinator (Academic Coordinator I) - Benioff Ocean Science Laboratory, Marine Science Institute, UCSB (JPF03166) - Recruit Skip to main content Recruit Home Open Recruitments Education and Science Engagement Coordinator (Academic Coordinator I) - Benioff Ocean Science Laboratory, Marine Science Institute, UCSB (JPF03166) Education and Science Engagement Coordinator (Academic Coordinator I) - Benioff Ocean Science Laboratory, Marine Science Institute, UCSB Apply now to Education and Science Engagement Coordinator (Academic Coordinator I) - Benioff Ocean Science Laboratory, Marine Science Institute, UCSB Job #JPF03166 Marine Science Institute / Office of Research / UC Santa Barbara Position overview Position title: Education and Science Engagement Coordinator Salary range: A reasonable estimate for this position at 100% time is $66,798-$83,469 Percent time: 100% Anticipated start: Position start date is negotiable and ideally anticipated to start in January 2027. Application Window Open date: September 23, 2026 Next review date: Friday, Oct 23, 2026 at 11:59pm (Pacific Time) Apply by this date to ensure full consideration by the committee. Final date: Saturday, May 1, 2027 at 11:59pm (Pacific Time) Applications will continue to be accepted until this date, but those received after the review date will only be considered if the position has not yet been filled. Position description The Benioff Ocean Science Laboratory is an applied research center at the University of California Santa Barbara’s Marine Science Institute. The Benioff Ocean Science Laboratory team works to leverage the power of science and technology to develop and implement tangible solutions to a wide range of ocean and environmental problems. The Benioff Ocean Science Laboratory seeks an Education and Science Engagement Coordinator to administer education and science engagement programming at local schools and community organizations in the Los Angeles/Long Beach area, in particular near the San Pedro port complex. The Coordinator will develop, manage, and facilitate the programming, which will primarily leverage novel air quality data and information generated by a regional network of air sensors operated by the Benioff Ocean Science Laboratory and UC Berkeley. Many of the sensors will be located on or near school properties where the programming will be taking place. The goal will be to promote STEM learning centered around the topic of air quality science including understanding data, environmental science, public health, and community science. Hands-on education modules will be developed by the Coordinator with support from university subject matter experts and will build upon and adapt existing lessons and resources for an informal classroom setting, such as after school, extracurricular opportunities for high school students. Specific lesson subjects may reflect the interests and strengths of the successful applicant, but might focus on data analysis, basic coding and/or AI-assisted coding, the links between environmental health and human health, environmental justice, the fundamentals of air quality and chemistry, the basics of environmental policy, the history of air pollution, among other potential topics. (Note: This role does not involve the development of standards-aligned semester- or year-long curriculum.) The Coordinator will be responsible for delivering and facilitating the modules; building strong relationships with local schools, communities, and organizations; and managing the logistical details of on-the-ground program implementation. The Education and Science Engagement Coordinator position will require frequent local travel and commutes to work with students and teachers at their respective schools in the San Pedro port complex area. Building strong and trusting relationships with local community organizations among other stakeholders will be essential. The Coordinator is expected to manage the day-to-day administrative details of the program, and the development and implementation of the programming with support from the UC Santa Barbara and UC Berkeley teams. As time allows, the Coordinator may contribute to education and outreach programs for other Benioff Ocean Science Laboratory projects. Occasional travel to UC Santa Barbara to meet with program leads will also be required. In summary, the Education and Science Engagement Coordinator will have the following responsibilities: ● Building on existing materials, develop informal after school (i.e., not standard-aligned) education modules and content for high school students. Materials will focus on subjects related to environmental data science, air quality, and public health and will be developed with support from UC Santa Barbara and Berkeley research teams ● Administer education programming and program delivery to students, teachers, and communities ● Collaborate with community and school groups to organize events, field trips, and other activities ● Build and manage partnerships with a variety of stakeholders, such as school and school district representatives, principals, teachers, students, community organizations, and local residents ● Manage logistics, budgets, and administrative tasks associated with education and science engagement programs related to the project ● Pursue creative opportunities and ideas to improve and expand programs and initiatives ● Provide basic support to research activities associated with the air sensor network and act as a community liaison on behalf of UC Santa Barbara and UC Berkeley scientists to inform the applied use and visualization of air quality sensor data ● Advise on and support other marine and environmental science community outreach and education programs conducted by the Benioff Ocean Science Laboratory, as time allows. This appointment includes a competitive benefits package with medical, dental and vision insurance and a UC retirement plan. Salary is commensurate with the applicant’s qualifications and experience. Department : https://msi.ucsb.edu/ Division : https://bosl.ucsb.edu/ Qualifications Basic qualifications (required at time of application) A bachelor’s degree in education, public health, environmental science, communications, or related field is required at the time of application. Additional qualifications (required at time of start) At least 3 years of relevant professional experience and/or a master’s degree, or relevant certifications in education, public health, environmental science, communications, or other related fields is required at the time of appointment. Requirements to work with students in public schools (e.g., fingerprint screening, TB test, etc.) must be completed at the time of appointment. Preferred qualifications ● Experience developing and creating education modules, activity guides, session plans, and materials, preferably with a STEM and or computer/data science focus and familiarity with data analysis and/or coding ● Experience teaching and facilitating educational lessons, activities, and events in formal and/or informal settings ● Track record of building and managing trusting relationships with schools, community organizations, or similar ● Self-motivated with a demonstrated ability to independently manage several responsibilities, schedules, and deadlines simultaneously ● Excellent written and verbal communication skills ● State teaching certification/credential ● Interest in science, technology, and/or public health; however, no formal experience in these fields is required ● Proficiency in Spanish a plus. Application Requirements Document requirements Curriculum Vitae -   Your most recently updated C.V. Cover Letter Work Samples (1) -   Samples may include education materials, lesson plans, or other related work products that the applicant developed independently or led the development of in a current or previous professional or volunteer capacity Work Samples (2)  -   Samples may include education materials, lesson plans, or other related work products that the applicant developed independently or led the development of in a current or previous professional or volunteer capacity (Optional) Reference requirements 3 required (contact information only) The search committee will contact references of the top candidate(s) after the interviews. After references are checked, a top candidate will be chosen. Apply link: https://recruit.ap.ucsb.edu/JPF03166 Help contact: lswanson@ucsb.edu About UC Santa Barbara As a condition of employment, the finalist will be required to disclose if they are subject to any final administrative or judicial decisions within the last seven years determining that they committed any misconduct. “Misconduct” means any violation of the policies or laws governing conduct at the applicant’s previous place of employment, including, but not limited to, violations of policies or laws prohibiting sexual harassment, sexual assault, or other forms of harassment or discrimination as defined by the employer. UC Sexual Violence and Sexual Harassment Policy UC Anti-Discrimination Policy for Employees, students and third parties APM - 035: Affirmative Action and Nondiscrimination in Employment Additionally, you will be required to comply with the University of California Policy on Vaccination Programs , as may be amended or revised from time to time.  Federal, state, or local public health directives may impose additional requirements. The University of California is an Equal Opportunity Employer. All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, national origin, disability, age, protected veteran status, or other protected status under state or federal law. Job location Los Angeles/Long Beach area, in particular near the San Pedro port complex Apply now to Education and Science Engagement Coordinator (Academic Coordinator I) - Benioff Ocean Science Laboratory, Marine Science Institute, UCSB or Log in to your portfolio Need help? Contact the hiring department . The University of California Santa Barbara is an Equal Opportunity/Affirmative Action Employer. You have the right to an equal employment opportunity . The University of California Santa Barbara is committed to providing reasonable accommodations to applicants with disabilities . See our Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act Annual Security Reports Academic Personnel Recruit University of California Santa Barbara Accessibility About California Consumer Privacy Act (CCPA) Opt-Out Icon Privacy Policy © 2007-2026 The Regents of the University of California. All rights reserved."
