    "https://recruit.ap.ucsb.edu/JPF03159": {
      "title": "Assistant Teaching Professor",
      "description": "Assistant Teaching Professor - Chemistry and Biochemistry (JPF03159) - Recruit Skip to main content Recruit Home Open Recruitments Assistant Teaching Professor - Chemistry and Biochemistry (JPF03159) Assistant Teaching Professor - Chemistry and Biochemistry Apply now to Assistant Teaching Professor - Chemistry and Biochemistry Job #JPF03159 College of Creative Studies / College of Creative Studies / UC Santa Barbara Position overview Position title: Assistant Teaching Professor Salary range: A reasonable estimate for this position is $88,300 - $160,000. Percent time: 100% (50% Department of Chemistry and Biochemistry, 50% College of Creative Studies) Anticipated start: July 1, 2027, or later Application Window Open date: October 12, 2026 Next review date: Wednesday, Nov 11, 2026 at 11:59pm (Pacific Time) Apply by this date to ensure full consideration by the committee. Final date: Wednesday, Mar 31, 2027 at 11:59pm (Pacific Time) Applications will continue to be accepted until this date, but those received after the review date will only be considered if the position has not yet been filled. Position description The College of Creative Studies (CCS) and the Department of Chemistry and Biochemistry (DCB) in the College of Letters and Science at the University of California Santa Barbara jointly invite applications for a security-of-employment-track (analogous to tenure track) faculty position as an Assistant Teaching Professor, with an anticipated appointment start date of July 1, 2027 or later, and course instruction anticipated to start Fall 2027 or later. The appointment will be evenly split between CCS and DCB. Faculty in this title are members of the Academic Senate, with benefits and privileges that parallel the traditional professor track. This 100% (full time - academic year) position is eligible for full University of California benefits. CCS and DCB are looking for individuals with expertise in biochemistry, capable of teaching both lower- and upper-division undergraduate classes, including upper-division biochemistry laboratory courses. Mentorship of undergraduate students is also a very significant component of the position. In addition to teaching and teaching-related activities and mentorship, additional responsibilities of UC teaching professor series faculty include professional and/or scholarly achievement and creative activity and participation in university and public service. Additional duties will include general advising, student admissions, and program coordination and assessment. The successful candidate will work with faculty colleagues and staff to enhance curriculum, course design, and pedagogy and contribute to efforts to update courses through evidence-based pedagogical approaches. In addition, they will develop a program with scholarly impact based on engagement in pedagogical professional development, discipline-based education research, instructional innovation, or research in the underlying discipline. The successful candidate will also contribute to the department’s climate through a record of productive collaborations and positive engagement with colleagues and peers. CCS is a small undergraduate college designed for self-directed students in the sciences and humanities within a world-class research university. It maintains a strong focus on undergraduate research and creative activities, presently hosts nine majors, and offers B.S. and B.A. degrees in Chemistry and Biochemistry. Further information on CCS can be found on the CCS website . DCB is a large department in the Division of Life, Math and Physical Sciences in the College of Letters and Sciences, and offers undergraduate B.S. and B.A. degrees in Chemistry and a B.S. in Biochemistry. DCB also confers M.A. and Ph.D. degrees. Further information on DCB can be found on the DCB website . Unit : https://www.ccs.ucsb.edu Department : https://www.chem.ucsb.edu Qualifications Basic qualifications (required at time of application) Applicants must have completed all requirements for a Ph.D. in Chemistry, Biochemistry or closely related field except the dissertation at the time of the application. Additional qualifications (required at time of start) Ph.D. in Chemistry, Biochemistry or closely related field and have a minimum of one year of teaching experience (teaching assistant experience is counted), by the time of appointment. Preferred qualifications Preferred candidates will be skilled instructors with experience in classroom and laboratory teaching, who are familiar with evidence-based pedagogy and learning assessment methodologies and are innovative and entrepreneurial with respect to developing instructional initiatives. A demonstrable commitment to teaching excellence is essential. Preferred candidates will have a strong potential for scholarly activities with broad impact in pedagogical professional development, discipline-based education research, instructional innovation, or research in the underlying discipline. Application Requirements Document requirements Curriculum Vitae -   An up-to-date C.V., preferably including a listing by term of your teaching experience. Cover Letter -   Please concisely introduce us to your teaching experience and highlight your unique qualifications for this position, preferably on a single page with reasonable formatting (example: 12 pt font, 1-inch margins). Statement of Teaching Experience and Mentoring Philosophy -   (preferably 2-page maximum with reasonable formatting) - This document should outline your teaching philosophy and approaches to instruction and mentoring, and may include, but not limited to, detailed teaching experience, career goals as a teacher, preferred courses to teach, and how your past instructional duties or training inform your teaching approaches and use of evidence-based instructional techniques. Statement of Professional and/or Scholarly Achievement and Creative Activity -   Please describe current or proposed professional and/or scholarly achievement and creative activity. For example, original research in the underlying discipline, advancing science education at or beyond UCSB, or K-12 community outreach. (preferably 2 page maximum with reasonable formatting). (Optional) Reference requirements 3-5 letters of reference required Applicants should provide the names and contact information of 3-5 references and must request letters of reference within the system in order to complete application requirements. Apply link: https://recruit.ap.ucsb.edu/JPF03159 Help contact: search@ccs.ucsb.edu About UC Santa Barbara As a condition of employment, the finalist will be required to disclose if they are subject to any final administrative or judicial decisions within the last seven years determining that they committed any misconduct. “Misconduct” means any violation of the policies or laws governing conduct at the applicant’s previous place of employment, including, but not limited to, violations of policies or laws prohibiting sexual harassment, sexual assault, or other forms of harassment or discrimination as defined by the employer. UC Sexual Violence and Sexual Harassment Policy UC Anti-Discrimination Policy for Employees, students and third parties APM - 035: Affirmative Action and Nondiscrimination in Employment Additionally, you will be required to comply with the University of California Policy on Vaccination Programs , as may be amended or revised from time to time.  Federal, state, or local public health directives may impose additional requirements. The University of California is an Equal Opportunity Employer. All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, national origin, disability, age, protected veteran status, or other protected status under state or federal law. Job location Santa Barbara, CA (work will be onsite) Apply now to Assistant Teaching Professor - Chemistry and Biochemistry or Log in to your portfolio Need help? Contact the hiring department . The University of California Santa Barbara is an Equal Opportunity/Affirmative Action Employer. You have the right to an equal employment opportunity . The University of California Santa Barbara is committed to providing reasonable accommodations to applicants with disabilities . See our Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act Annual Security Reports Academic Personnel Recruit University of California Santa Barbara Accessibility About California Consumer Privacy Act (CCPA) Opt-Out Icon Privacy Policy © 2007-2026 The Regents of the University of California. All rights reserved."
